{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "cpsthub",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-04-26",
  "pages_build_output_dir": "./dist",
  "compatibility_flags": [
    "nodejs_compat"
  ]

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------
  // DEPLOY NOTE — project "cpsthub"
  //
  // Env vars / secrets are ALREADY configured on the existing Pages project
  // (production environment) and are intentionally NOT redeclared here, so the
  // deploy inherits them without the "Binding name already in use" collision
  // and without clearing anything:
  //
  //   env vars (plain_text):  GOOGLE_API_KEY, DRIVE_FOLDER_ID
  //   secrets  (encrypted):   SESSION_SECRET, ADMIN_SEED_EMAIL, ADMIN_SEED_PASSWORD
  //
  // REQUIRED for PRIVATE Drive files — GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON:
  //   The full Google Service Account JSON key, stored as an ENCRYPTED SECRET on
  //   the Pages project (production environment), on a single line:
  //
  //     npx wrangler pages secret put GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON --project-name cpsthub
  //
  //   src/lib/drive.ts exchanges it for an OAuth2 bearer token (RS256-signed JWT
  //   via Web Crypto — see src/lib/google-auth.ts) with scope
  //   https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.readonly, so the app authenticates
  //   as a real Drive principal and PRIVATE files display. Share the library
  //   folder (and the secondary folder) with the key's `client_email` — Viewer
  //   access is sufficient. GOOGLE_API_KEY is used ONLY as a fallback when this
  //   secret is absent (public files only). It is deliberately NOT declared as a
  //   binding here so the deploy inherits the dashboard/secret value without a
  //   "Binding name already in use" collision, exactly like the vars above.
  //   Read server-side only; NEVER logged, NEVER sent to the browser.
  //
  // OPTIONAL: SECONDARY_DRIVE_FOLDER_ID — an additional allowed Drive folder
  // that holds the real targets of the shortcut items placed in the main
  // folder. If not set on the Pages project, the app falls back to the known
  // folder id baked into src/lib/drive.ts (driveSecondaryId), so shortcuts
  // keep resolving without any dashboard change. Read server-side only.
  //
  // All are read server-side only and NEVER sent to the browser. For local dev
  // the same names live in `.dev.vars` (see .dev.vars.example).
  //
  // The D1 binding MUST be declared here: unlike dashboard env vars, a Pages
  // deploy does NOT auto-inherit the D1 binding, so omitting it produces a
  // deployment with no DB (login → AUTH_UNAVAILABLE). It points at the EXISTING
  // production database — no new database is created.
  //
  // KV namespaces ARE now bound (see "kv_namespaces" below) — this activates the
  // caching layer that was already written in the code, with zero code changes:
  //
  //   DRIVE_CACHE — Drive folder-listing cache read/written by src/lib/drive.ts.
  //     Without this binding drive.ts falls back to a per-isolate in-memory Map
  //     that is constantly cold, so nearly every folder navigation triggered a
  //     live Google Drive round-trip.
  //   SESSIONS    — hot-path session cache used by src/lib/session.ts,
  //     src/routes/auth.ts and src/lib/google-oauth.ts. D1 remains the source of
  //     truth for sessions; KV only avoids the DB hit on every request.
  //
  // Binding names must stay EXACTLY "DRIVE_CACHE" and "SESSIONS" — the code reads
  // env.DRIVE_CACHE / env.SESSIONS verbatim. KV namespace ids are not secrets.
  // ------------------------------------------------------------------
  // REMOVED ON PURPOSE: the "d1_databases" and "kv_namespaces" blocks (DB, DRIVE_CACHE, SESSIONS) previously bound the live production resources and were deleted so this project cannot deploy to or write to them; they must be re-created against NEW resources owned by this project before any deploy.
}
